## The one compiler change: unbound type variables close to Integer

`IR/ResolveTypes.codex` (COMPILER-81, our issue 146). `resolve-ty-deep` now
maps every `TypeVar` that the definition's own signature does not carry to
`int-default`. The IR pipeline, and so `inline-single-caller`, runs BEFORE
`rewrite-ir-defs` (opening.codex:772 then :851).

Five probes, `codexir | zigemit | zig`, in the session scratchpad `u61probe/`:

    probe                  U60 native      U61 native      U61 SEED (bare metal) -> zigemit
    p1 issue 146           refused T287    refused T287    ok ok  (int-default)
    p2 nullary at Real     correct         correct
    p3 inlined generic     correct         correct
    p4 empty lists         refused         refused         refused: "no zig type" (error)
    p5 local lambdas       correct         correct

**Our native codexir cannot see the fix, and that is OURS.** The harness is
generated with `frontend_source(..., resolve=False)`
(cobblestone-qemu `subjects/gen_codexir_harness.py:92`). `emit_harness.py`'s
docstring justifies that: RESOLVE "lives in compile-frontend-cdx ONLY --
compile-frontend-ir ... emits the IR with its annotations unrewritten". That has
been false since Update 50 (`8cc80685`), when the resolve moved into
`compile-frontend-passes`, which both entries call. It is the only harness of
that shape: codex-zig-transpiler's and codex-wasm-transpiler's call the driver
(`compile-frontend-ir`, `-cdx`) and so resolve once rebuilt at U61. Until U61
the phase was a no-op on what we compared (a record
program, p6, is IDENTICAL seed vs native), so nothing showed it.

Consequence: our IR oracle (`~/codexir`, the curated `.upstream.ir` freezes,
the Rust arm's grading) and `codexzig` diverge from the seed wherever an unbound
variable survives lowering. Curated `run-zig` will keep refusing
`roc-poly-nopayload-variant` at U61 although upstream's pipeline accepts it.

## A counterexample to COMPILER-30's closed invariant

COMPILER-30 records "a clean compile carries zero `ErrorTy`" as MEASURED and
HOLDING (red, 2026-09-08). `list-length [[]]` compiles clean on the U61 seed
and carries `(list-expr (elems) error)`. Minimal:

    [[]]        error on the inner literal, outer element left as a bare tvar
    [[[]]]      the same, one level down
    [[1]]  [[], [1]]  [[1], []]  ([] : List (List Integer))     clean

At U61 the bare outer variable is then CLOSED TO INTEGER. The seed's IR types
`[[]]` as `List Integer` and `list-length` as `List Integer -> Integer`, which
is not an arbitrary choice for an unconstrained variable -- the element IS a
list. The `error` predates U61 (U60 native shows it); the wrong Integer is new.

**Attributed, and not ours.** The root is the checker: `infer-list` types an
empty literal as a bare fresh variable, not `List t`
(`Types/TypeCheckerInference.codex:1386-1390`) -- plausibly so that `[]` can be
a List or a LinkedList, which `empty-list-element-source` serves. Nothing
constrains the variable to be a list at all, so in `[[]]` the element type is
free, lowering finds no list type for the inner literal and falls to
`IrList [] ErrorTy` (`Lowering.codex:1456`, the arm upstream's row names as
pre-dating PR 101). Our PR 101 added the lookup under the literal's span; it
does not reach this case, and did not cause it.

The same looseness is a SOUNDNESS HOLE, native codexir, no diagnostics:

    v : Integer = []          clean, IR (list-expr (elems) error)
    text-length []            clean
    [] + 1                    clean, IR carries (error "arithmetic operand ...")
    [1] + 1                   CDX2001 List vs Integer   (the control)

To confirm on the seed (one ~10 s compile) before filing as an issue; the
List/LinkedList design is theirs to rule on.

